so let me start by saying  congratulations on the movie  um i am curious what is it like then  for you like the night before you're  gonna maybe meet  matt damon and tom for the first time to  possibly talk about the movie  you know before you were officially  signed on if you will i i don't know how  it all worked  but what is it like for you the night  before you're going to meet people like  that are you in your head a little bit  you know so i i it was in  different steps first i had to drink  with tom  and the two co-writers and then  i hoped i would be given the opportunity  to audition  but no news during a month a month and a  half so i thought  and i hadn't read the movie by then uh  so i thought oh  okay well no i'm not auditioning and  then  a month and a half after they sent me  the script  and i could audition and then i started  working  really hard and  to be honest the work was to get into  that story  as as as much as i could believe like i  remember i had to audition  the scene when he comes in and i had i  even tried to  see the furniture the kitchen imagine  everything my apartment my life in  marseille  tried to come with as much material as i  could  from the story to be in my mind so that  and then when i met tom when i  auditioned he was here  and then we worked together for an hour  and it was great i  i really enjoyed the audition i was  really stressed  but at the same time working with tom  was  fantastic because it was it was  you know tommy's an actor and i think  he's done a lot of theater and i've done  a lot of theater too like the character  i come from  theater um so it really reminded me like  the rehearsals when you really sweat  and tried to find something and and i  thought when i came out i thought all  right it might not be me  it doesn't depend on me but the moment i  was happy because i thought i  i i i had you know  i've done what i could and and and then  i enjoyed the moment  and then i was confirmed and then i met  matt  and then yes i had trouble falling  asleep and  and then uh it took me a little while to  relax but uh the thing is  as he he his performance is incredible  he disappeared behind bill baker  so i at matt damon i just you know  had a vague memory of matt damon but  he disappeared yeah he's what we call a  talented actor  um yeah yeah we could call him that  yeah exactly i'm just gonna go with that  um i love learning about the behind the  scenes  uh of the making of a movie what do you  think might surprise people to learn  about the making of stillwater  well honestly i i was often asked is it  so what do you think about working in an  american movie is it really different  and it wasn't  it was really like so close to well like  for example if you like the behind the  scene matt damon  is it like a very how does he behave as  a hollywood star  so when we were shooting in my flats we  had the other flats  being like for the green room and also  where the monitors were  so tom was just in this little room with  his monitor  and then matt his green room was a  kitchen a very tiny kitchen  and you could only have one chair in it  and it was all long so he was sitting on  a high  high chair and the door was always open  and then  you had like his friends from hair and  and makeup standing there but they  couldn't even  sit and so we were very far away from  the  the image that we have of a movie star  you know he was very simple uh one of  the things i think that adds to this  film  uh is that you guys shot on location  there were no sound stages  um you know and i think that adds so  much to the movie uh can you sort of  talk about  filming on location and what that you  know what that meant to you  yes of course uh i think marseille is a  character  and she's it's one of the main character  of the movie  and so the way he filmed marseille was  just so  it was realistic and artistic and  and at the same time the for example in  our flat the setting  it was the ambience in the flat the  atmosphere was  was filled with the mediterranean energy  and it really helped  and um so  definitely we were all  filled with the energy of the city and  and  and the actors there were a lot of local  actors lilu she lives in marseille  uh renault also he lives in the south of  france  musa there are many actors who who live  there so  it definitely helped um feeling it was a  reality  completely uh uh i am what we call  excited  for uh some movie called um the house of  gucci i think it was made by  ridley scott i think i'm familiar i  think he's made some movies before  um yeah can you sort of talk about  getting to work with another really  gifted director and what that experience  was like  yeah it was it was really it was really  a beautiful experience and then i  again my partners were quite impressive  and i think they they've delivered  amazing performances  i can't wait to see the movie because i  think they are going to be very  impressive  yeah i saw um i was able to talk to  ridley when he was in italy  and he showed me it was not just me but  he showed his italian villa where he was  put up with these  statues and it just looked like he was  having the time of his life he's so  energetic about everything  he is yes he's yeah you just described  it perfectly he has  a pleasure that is like pure  pure pleasure and joy of doing what he  does  which is impressive because his energies  beyond what we can imagine completely my  last question for you um about  stillwater  one of the things that i really love  about about tom's work is that  his dialogue just feels authentic and  real it doesn't feel like it's out of  some hollywood movie can you sort of  talk about uh  just the dialogue and the script and the  quality of the writing  it's funny because i it was speaking  english speaking french was just  didn't really matter because the  dialogues were so close to  what was happening between the  characters that  they were they became uh  just it's not it's not a dialect that  that sometimes words can be obstacles  but they were  just um tools  i would say sure um  on that note i gotta stop i'm just gonna  say congrats on the movie i wish you  nothing but the best thank you for your  time  thank you bye-bye

